# “The Wreck Beneath” — A Dream Run Through the Floor Ocean

(Hyperreal cinematic — surreal physics / ocean-floor atmosphere / dream logic)

## Narrative Arc (15 s)

A young woman runs barefoot across an endless mirrored floor where the ocean’s surface replaces the ground. Beneath her, waves move in slow motion; above, refracted sunlight paints her sheer gown. She runs toward a sunken wooden ship visible under the translucent water. As she nears it, time bends—the floor ripples, gravity inverts—and she dives into the reflection, merging sky and sea.


## Style/look

Visual Style: Hyper-realistic 8K cinematic; emulates 65 mm photochemical film with fine-grain texture and restrained halation on highlights.
Art Direction: Denis Villeneuve-like austerity meets ethereal dreamscapes; realism first, surrealism through physics, not filters.
Tone: Mystical, sacred, emotional; blends weight and grace.
Lens Kit: 28 mm, 50 mm, 85 mm primes; Black Pro-Mist ¼ filter.
Color Grade: Teal–amber dual tone, lifted blacks for haze retention, soft highlight roll-off; underwater caustics rendered in pale gold.
Lighting Logic: Low-angle dawn sunlight with volumetric fog; cool ambient bounce from water surface.
Texture Qualities: Satin sheen on sheer gown; subsurface skin scattering; micro-droplets on air film; wet-glass reflections.
Camera Grammar: True-to-life optics (28 mm / 50 mm / 85 mm primes), Pro-Mist ¼ filtration, no artificial depth exaggeration.
Mood Continuity: From calm → resistance → freedom → awe → transcendence.
Sound Design: Diegetic water steps, low rumble, breath, faint piano sting.


## Format / Capture

Duration: 15 s @ 30 fps
Aspect: 9:16
Shutter: 180°
Capture Emulation: Digital-to-film LUT (Kodak 5207 base)
Finishing: Fine-grain overlay; mild chroma noise; cinematic LUT with warm–cool split.

## Physics / Environment

Setting: “The floor ocean” — an infinite reflective surface of shallow water behaving as a boundary between air and sea.
Medium Transition: Atmosphere thickens around subject; viscosity interpolates from 0.6× air to 3× water during first half of Scene 2.
Particle Behavior: Subsurface scattering, volumetric mist, micro-bubbles suspended in air; gravity inversion in final dive.
Cloth Dynamics: Sheer silk gown with variable drag coefficient (0.8 → 0.2); soft follow-through and curl on edges.
Interaction: Ripples, delayed refraction, floating droplets that hang before falling.
FX Integrity: No cartoon exaggeration; all motion grounded in plausible fluid dynamics.

## Sound Design

Diegetic: Barefoot impacts on liquid surface, distant thunder, heartbeat resonance, soft inhale/exhale.
Transition: Underwater muffled acoustics → open atmospheric rush → silence + single piano note (C♯ minor).
Spatial Mix: Wide stereo with low-frequency reverb tail (~5 s).
No score until final note.

## Scene Breakdown

Scene 1 — The Awakening Floor (0–3 s)
Mood: Calm reverence, first breath of the dream.

Action: The woman stands barefoot on the glassy ocean-floor. Her gown sways in invisible currents; reflections ripple beneath her feet as if the sky were water.
Camera: Low wide shot (28 mm); dolly forward at slow pace.
Physics: Gentle oscillation of fabric and light as if suspended between air and liquid.
Lighting: Dawn tones—golden-blue split, volumetric haze.

Scene 2 — The Struggle and Release (3–6 s)
Mood: Determination evolving into liberation.

Action:
She starts to run, but movement is sluggish—her limbs cut through a viscous medium; every gesture trails with silky drag.
Gradually, resistance fades. Her speed builds; each step releases misty bursts and water-light ripples that chase behind her.
By the end of the scene, she’s moving freely—running through atmosphere as though gravity itself loosens its grip.
Camera: Side tracking shot (50 mm), handheld for realism; subtle motion blur emphasizing effort → steadier glide as she accelerates.
Physics: Air density transition—micro-particles drift, delay, then dissolve. Fabric dynamics interpolate from heavy drag → light float.
Lighting: Sunbeams scatter through fog; caustic reflections animate underfoot.
Sound Cue: Muffled heartbeat and deep thuds (underwater acoustics) shifting to open atmospheric rush and echoing breath.

Scene 3 — The Reveal (6–9 s)
Mood: Discovery and awe.

Action: She slows—below the translucent floor, the silhouette of a sunken wooden ship emerges, bathed in amber light.
Camera: Overhead crane tilt-down, 35 mm; slow focus pull from her to the ship.
Physics: Ripples distort the ship’s form, shimmering like memory.
Lighting: Cyan depths mixed with golden shafts; bubbles drift upward beneath the floor.

Scene 4 — The Breach (9–12 s)
Mood: Epiphany.

Action: She kneels and touches the ocean-floor. The reflection liquefies outward from her fingers, bending reality.
Camera: Close-up (85 mm macro); shallow depth; slow focus breathing.
Physics: Surface tension breaks and reforms; inverted gravity particles float upward.
FX: Refraction warps her face reflection; droplets hang in air.
Sound Cue: Low-frequency hum, faint heartbeat slowing, subtle chime of contact.


Scene 5 — The Dive (12–15 s)
Mood: Transcendence; dream becomes reality.

Action: She takes a breath and dives forward into the reflection. Sky and sea invert—she falls upward while the ship ascends toward her.
Camera: Overhead rotation at high speed, transitioning into orbiting aerial shot; silhouette merges with rising wreck.
Physics: Bubble trails spiral upward; fabric flows like a comet tail.
Lighting: Blinding white burst → fades to calm subaquatic blue.
Sound: Silence, then single resonant piano note echoing through water.

## Cue Notes

Poster frame: Scene 5, half-inverted, gown flowing, reflection tearing apart in light.
Keep subject continuity (face, gown, lighting) across scenes.
Avoid overt CGI look—retain natural fluid physics, filmic exposure, and lens imperfections (minor bloom, dust motes).
Maintain rhythmic pacing: 3 s beats with clear visual transitions at 3 / 6 / 9 / 12 s.



